---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/hidden-rivers.css';
import katex from 'katex';
const math=(s:string)=>katex.renderToString(s,{throwOnError:false,displayMode:true});
---
<Base wide title="Hidden Rivers | ocean transport and coastal representations" description="Trace modeled ocean transport, inspect rotation and finite-time stretching, and compare coastal satellite representations in a linked 3D atlas." pageClass="hidden-rivers-page">
<article class="ocean-observatory" data-surface="ocean">
  <section class="ocean-stage" id="explorer" aria-label="Ocean transport observatory">
    <div id="ocean-canvas" class="ocean-canvas" role="img" aria-label="Interactive geospatial ocean-current visualization. Use the controls or location inspector below."></div>
    <div class="stage-shade" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="stage-heading"><p class="eyebrow">IAN HELFRICH <span>/</span> OCEANOGRAPHIC SYSTEMS</p><h1>Hidden<br/><em>Rivers</em></h1><p class="stage-deck">Ocean transport.<br/>Seen through motion.</p><p class="stage-evidence" id="ocean-evidence">HYCOM · NOAA · Tessera · Cesium</p></div>
    <div class="stage-topline"><div class="region-switch" role="group" aria-label="Ocean region"><button type="button" data-region="agulhas" aria-pressed="true"><span>01</span> Agulhas</button><button type="button" data-region="bahamas" aria-pressed="false"><span>02</span> Florida</button><button type="button" data-region="denmark" aria-pressed="false"><span>03</span> Denmark Strait</button></div><a class="stage-archive" href="/projects/hidden-rivers">Project record ↗</a></div>
    <div class="stage-location"><span class="location-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span><div><span id="ocean-region-title">Agulhas retroflection</span><small id="ocean-bounds">SOUTH ATLANTIC / INDIAN OCEAN</small></div></div>
    <div class="stage-tools"><button type="button" id="ocean-view" aria-pressed="false">Oblique view</button><button type="button" id="ocean-reset">Fit region</button><button type="button" id="ocean-image">Save image ↗</button><button type="button" id="ocean-share">Share view ↗</button></div>
    <div class="stage-legend"><span id="ocean-legend-title">Horizontal speed</span><div id="ocean-gradient"></div><div class="legend-ticks" id="ocean-legend-ticks"><span>0</span><span>1</span><span>2 m/s</span></div><small id="ocean-legend-note">Color encodes speed. Light does not encode water volume.</small></div>
    <div class="stage-loading" id="ocean-status" role="status"><span class="loading-line" aria-hidden="true"></span><p>Loading ocean fields</p></div>
    <div class="stage-caption"><span id="ocean-path-info">Deterministic model trajectories</span><span id="ocean-camera-note">Drag to pan · Scroll to zoom · Click to inspect</span><span class="imagery-credit">Imagery © Esri, Maxar, Earthstar Geographics</span></div>
    <div class="stage-bottom">
      <div class="mode-switch" role="group" aria-label="Analysis layer"><button type="button" data-mode="flow" aria-pressed="true"><span>01</span>Transport<small>Particle paths</small></button><button type="button" data-mode="vorticity" aria-pressed="false"><span>02</span>Rotation<small>Signed vorticity</small></button><button type="button" data-mode="stretching" aria-pressed="false"><span>03</span>Deformation<small>48-hour stretching</small></button><button type="button" data-mode="column" aria-pressed="false"><span>04</span>Depth<small>Aligned layers</small></button></div>
      <div class="playback-row"><button id="ocean-play" class="play-button" type="button" aria-pressed="true">Pause</button><div class="time-control"><div><output id="ocean-date">2 October 2026, 00:00 UTC</output><span id="ocean-cadence">Source cadence</span></div><input id="ocean-time" type="range" min="0" max="432000" step="900" value="259200" aria-label="Model time"/><div class="time-extents"><span>29 SEPTEMBER</span><span id="ocean-time-end">4 OCTOBER 2026</span></div></div><div class="depth-switch" id="ocean-depths" role="group" aria-label="Current depth"><button data-depth="0" type="button">Surface</button><button data-depth="200" type="button" aria-pressed="true">200 m</button><button data-depth="1000" type="button">1,000 m</button></div></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="observatory-nav"><a href="#explorer">01 / Ocean laboratory</a><a href="#coastal">02 / Coastal representations</a><a href="#cinema">03 / Film</a><a href="#methods">04 / Methods & evidence</a><span id="ocean-action-status" role="status"></span></div>
  <section class="analysis-reading" id="ocean-analysis" aria-labelledby="analysis-title">
    <div class="analysis-copy"><p class="eyebrow" id="ocean-mode-kicker">01 / LAGRANGIAN TRANSPORT</p><h2 id="analysis-title">Follow a parcel through a changing current.</h2><p id="ocean-mode-description">Each filament is a numerical trajectory through the model’s evolving horizontal velocity field. Its history is measured in ocean hours, independent of the display’s frame rate. Scrubbing time follows the same seeded particles.</p><p class="analysis-limit" id="ocean-mode-limit">Particles stay on a selected depth surface. These paths do not include vertical motion, turbulent diffusion, waves, or windage.</p><div class="analysis-links"><button type="button" id="ocean-film-camera">Slow camera orbit</button><a href="#methods">Inspect the calculation ↗</a></div></div>
    <div class="column-inspector"><div class="inspector-heading"><p class="eyebrow">VELOCITY / SELECTED LOCATION</p><h3 id="ocean-inspect-title">Choose a location in the ocean.</h3><p id="ocean-inspect-time"></p></div><form id="ocean-inspect-form"><label>Longitude °E<input type="number" step="any" id="ocean-lon" value="26" min="-180" max="180" required/></label><label>Latitude °N<input type="number" step="any" id="ocean-lat" value="-36" min="-90" max="90" required/></label><button type="submit">Inspect ↗</button></form><p id="ocean-inspect-message" role="status"></p><div class="inspect-table"><table><caption>Horizontal velocity · m/s</caption><thead><tr><th>Depth</th><th>Speed</th><th>East</th><th>North</th><th>Toward</th></tr></thead><tbody id="ocean-inspect-body"><tr><td colspan="5">Click the field above or enter coordinates.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><svg id="ocean-profile" role="img" aria-label="Modeled speed over time at the selected location"></svg><div id="ocean-profile-legend"></div><button id="ocean-csv" type="button" disabled>Download point series</button><p class="fineprint">Directions point toward motion, clockwise from true north. Missing values remain missing. Depth colors identify the plotted series; they do not encode speed.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section id="coastal" class="coastal-section">
    <header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / TESSERA REPRESENTATIONS · CAPE PENINSULA · 2024</p><h2>A coast in<br/><em>128 dimensions.</em></h2><div><p>Select a reference on the coast. Tessera’s full feature vector becomes a spatial query: which other locations resemble it in the satellite representation?</p><p>Use a second reference to inspect the difference. The colors show similarity or preference between references, with an explicit numerical scale.</p></div></header>
    <div id="coastal-microscope" aria-live="polite"><p class="coastal-placeholder">The coastal representation loads when this section enters view.</p></div>
    <div class="coastal-world"><div class="coastal-world-copy"><p class="eyebrow">THE SAME LOCATION / TERRAIN & BUILDINGS</p><h3>Return the representation<br/>to the landscape.</h3><p>A selected coastal pixel also supplies a geographic position. Open the 3D view to examine its terrain and available building geometry through Cesium ion.</p><button id="ocean-open-coast" type="button">Open 3D coast ↗</button><p id="coast-globe-status" role="status" class="fineprint">Uses your saved Cesium connection in this browser.</p><p class="fineprint">Building coverage varies. Terrain and buildings are separate context datasets; the Tessera representation summarizes 2024 and the ocean fields cover September–October 2026.</p></div><div class="coastal-globe-shell"><div id="coastal-globe" aria-label="Cesium terrain and available 3D buildings near the selected coastal location"><div class="globe-placeholder"><span>18.40° E<br/>34.20° S</span><small>CAPE PENINSULA / SOUTH AFRICA</small></div></div></div></div>
  </section>
  <section id="cinema" class="cinema-section"><header><p class="eyebrow">03 / DATA-DRIVEN FILM</p><h2>Motion through the field.</h2><p>Four chapters in 48 seconds: transport, rotation, deformation, and depth. The film uses the same numerical trajectories and diagnostic fields as the explorer.</p></header><div class="cinema-frame"><video id="ocean-film" controls preload="none" playsinline poster="/hidden-rivers/film-v3-poster.jpg"><source src="/hidden-rivers/film-v3.mp4" type="video/mp4"/><track kind="captions" src="/hidden-rivers/film-v3.vtt" srclang="en" label="English" default/></video></div><a id="ocean-film-download" href="/hidden-rivers/film-v3.mp4" download>Download the film ↗</a></section>
  <section id="methods" class="methods-section"><header><p class="eyebrow">04 / METHODS & EVIDENCE</p><h2>The picture has<br/><em>a numerical history.</em></h2></header><div class="method-intro"><p>The ocean model supplies eastward and northward velocity at geographic coordinates and depth. The atlas turns those fields into three distinct calculations. A path describes where a tracer travels; vorticity describes local rotation; finite-time stretching describes separation of nearby trajectories over a stated interval.</p><p>None is a direct observation of every parcel. The model grid, input cadence, missing values, and integration window constrain the result. Smaller integration steps improve the numerical calculation, not the resolving power of the source data.</p></div>
    <div class="method-ledger"><details open><summary><span>01</span> Geographic particle trajectories <span>+</span></summary><div class="method-body"><div set:html={math(String.raw`\frac{d\lambda}{dt}=\frac{u}{R\cos\phi},\qquad\frac{d\phi}{dt}=\frac{v}{R}`)}></div><p>Longitude λ and latitude φ are in radians; u and v are in metres per second. The renderer uses deterministic releases and a fixed physical sampling interval for trajectory history. The same model time therefore produces the same path geometry across devices. Masked cells and exits terminate trajectories.</p><p>Depth remains fixed. Particle number, glow, and line density aid visibility and have no interpretation as transported volume or probability. The 3D view exaggerates depth and uses independent NOAA relief, which can differ from the model’s wet mask near slopes.</p></div></details>
    <details><summary><span>02</span> Rotation on a sphere <span>+</span></summary><div class="method-body"><div set:html={math(String.raw`\zeta=\frac{1}{R\cos\phi}\left[\frac{\partial v}{\partial\lambda}-\frac{\partial(u\cos\phi)}{\partial\phi}\right]`)}></div><p>Relative vorticity has units of inverse seconds. Positive values describe counterclockwise local rotation; negative values describe clockwise rotation. A diverging palette places zero at its center. This diagnostic is a finite difference of the model field; it does not by itself identify a persistent eddy.</p></div></details>
    <details><summary><span>03</span> Finite-time stretching <span>+</span></summary><div class="method-body"><div set:html={math(String.raw`\sigma_T=\frac{1}{2|T|}\log\lambda_{\max}\!\left[(D\Phi_T)^\mathsf{T}D\Phi_T\right]`)}></div><p>The flow map Φ moves nearby initial locations through 48 hours of velocity data. Its spatial derivative measures how those locations separate. The largest stretching direction gives the forward finite-time Lyapunov exponent, reported per day. Trajectories without a valid full integration window are masked.</p><p>High stretching can arise from shear as well as features associated with coherent transport. A bright ridge is not proof of a transport barrier. The published diagnostics include numerical sensitivity checks; they do not quantify all ocean-model uncertainty.</p></div></details>
    <details><summary><span>04</span> Similarity across all Tessera dimensions <span>+</span></summary><div class="method-body"><div set:html={math(String.raw`s(a,x)=\frac{e_a^\mathsf{T}e_x}{\|e_a\|\,\|e_x\|},\qquad\Delta s=s(a,x)-s(b,x)`)}></div><p>Each vector contains 128 learned features. The microscope compares these vectors by cosine similarity. Positive contrast means the location resembles reference A more than B in this representation. It does not certify habitat type, ecological health, water depth, or current velocity.</p><p>The annual coastal representation and ocean analysis have different dates and purposes. Their link is geographic. No predictive or causal relationship between embeddings and currents is claimed.</p></div></details></div>
    <div class="sensitivity-study"><div><p class="eyebrow">A MEASURED LIMIT / AGULHAS SURFACE</p><h3>The sampling interval changes the answer.</h3><p>For the same 48-hour calculation, switching from three-hour inputs to daily samples changes the stretching field much more than halving the integration step. Small numerical error does not establish physical accuracy.</p><p>Absolute FTLE differences are in day⁻¹. Each comparison uses seed/window pairs valid in both runs across four release dates. These are sensitivity checks, not observational error estimates.</p></div><div class="sensitivity-table"><table><caption>Agulhas surface · absolute change in 48-hour FTLE</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Changed setting</th><th scope="col">Median</th><th scope="col">95th percentile</th><th scope="col">Pairs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Inputs: 3 hours → 24 hours</th><td>0.113692</td><td>0.427221</td><td>1,231</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">RK4 step: 900 s → 450 s</th><td>0.00000462</td><td>0.00005767</td><td>1,238</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Perturbation: ¼ → ⅛ cell</th><td>0.008695</td><td>0.056488</td><td>1,238</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Depth view uses daily inputs for all three layers. Surface-only transport and diagnostics retain the three-hour series. The four other velocity layers have daily inputs.</p></div></div>
    <div class="source-ledger"><div><h3>Inputs</h3><a href="https://www.hycom.org/dataserver/espc-d-v02/global-analysis">HYCOM / ESPC-D-V02 ↗</a><a href="https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/etopo180.html">NOAA ETOPO1 relief ↗</a><a href="https://geotessera.readthedocs.io/en/stable/">Tessera / GeoTessera ↗</a><a href="https://cesium.com/platform/cesium-ion/">Cesium ion terrain & buildings ↗</a></div><div><h3>Inspect the release</h3><a href="/hidden-rivers/data/manifest.json">Velocity metadata ↗</a><a href="/hidden-rivers/data/diagnostics.json">Diagnostic metadata ↗</a><a href="/hidden-rivers/data/source-requests.json">Daily source requests ↗</a><a href="/hidden-rivers/data/source-requests-3hour.json">Three-hour source requests ↗</a><a href="/hidden-rivers/coastal/manifest.json">Tessera provenance & validation ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/ihelfrich/ihelfrich.github.io/tree/main/scripts/hidden-rivers">Analysis, tests & rendering code ↗</a></div><div><h3>Interpretation</h3><a href="https://os.copernicus.org/articles/21/401/2025/">FTLE uncertainty: Matuszak et al. ↗</a><a href="https://www.whoi.edu/ocean-learning-hub/ocean-topics/how-the-ocean-works/ocean-circulation/currents-gyres-eddies/">WHOI: circulation and eddies ↗</a><p>Research visualization. Physical validation against colocated observations remains outside this release.</p></div></div>
  </section>
</article>
<script>import { startExpedition } from '../scripts/hidden-rivers/expedition.js';startExpedition();</script>
</Base>
